@api = http://localhost:5006
@receiver = http://localhost:5100
@tenant = acme

### What this sample is
GET {{api}}/

### Readiness - answers only once the container exists
GET {{api}}/health

### Place an order. The order document and its outbox record commit in one batch.
POST {{api}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenantId": "{{tenant}}",
  "customerId": "cust-1",
  "total": 42.50
}

### Both kinds of document, in the same container. That is what makes the write transactional.
GET {{api}}/container/{{tenant}}

### The outbox records themselves, with their status
GET {{api}}/outbox/{{tenant}}

### A batch that cannot commit. Expect HTTP 409.
POST {{api}}/orders/doomed
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenantId": "{{tenant}}",
  "customerId": "cust-doomed",
  "total": 99.00
}

### ...and nothing was written. Re-run these two: the counts are unchanged and the order is absent.
GET {{api}}/container/{{tenant}}

### Replace the id with the orderId the doomed call reported. Expect HTTP 404.
GET {{api}}/orders/{{tenant}}/paste-the-doomed-orderId-here

### What actually arrived at the receiver
GET {{receiver}}/deliveries

### Clear the receiver between runs
DELETE {{receiver}}/deliveries
